From: daedalao <daedalao@hotmail.com>
Date: Wed, 9 Sep 2026 16:44:37 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 13/16] runtime: drop SIGPROF samples taken inside emulated
 64-bit atomics on linux/ppc

linux/ppc emulates 64-bit atomics with a spinlock, like mips and arm. If
SIGPROF interrupts the critical section, sigprof's own atomic.Load64 in
profBuf.write spins forever on the same lock, inside the signal handler,
with all signals blocked. Apply the existing mips/arm workaround: count
the sample as lost and return.

Found by runtime/pprof TestGoroutineSwitch hanging on a PowerBook G4.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_0117Su2urNmg96oYM6vhHi1y
---
 src/runtime/proc.go | 4 ++--
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/runtime/proc.go b/src/runtime/proc.go
index 79f82c2ed4..6798bfd275 100644
--- a/src/runtime/proc.go
+++ b/src/runtime/proc.go
@@ -5809,13 +5809,13 @@ func sigprof(pc, sp, lr uintptr, gp *g, mp *m) {
 		return
 	}
 
-	// On mips{,le}/arm, 64bit atomics are emulated with spinlocks, in
+	// On mips{,le}/arm/ppc, 64bit atomics are emulated with spinlocks, in
 	// internal/runtime/atomic. If SIGPROF arrives while the program is inside
 	// the critical section, it creates a deadlock (when writing the sample).
 	// As a workaround, create a counter of SIGPROFs while in critical section
 	// to store the count, and pass it to sigprof.add() later when SIGPROF is
 	// received from somewhere else (with _LostSIGPROFDuringAtomic64 as pc).
-	if GOARCH == "mips" || GOARCH == "mipsle" || GOARCH == "arm" {
+	if GOARCH == "mips" || GOARCH == "mipsle" || GOARCH == "arm" || GOARCH == "ppc" {
 		if f := findfunc(pc); f.valid() {
 			if stringslite.HasPrefix(funcname(f), "internal/runtime/atomic") {
 				cpuprof.lostAtomic++
